{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Procedural Programming vs Object Oriented Programming"}, {"type": "position", "content": 1}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "A1.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use with programming languages."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Learning Outcome"}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Communicates effectively in English about the communicative topics for this level through the use of appropriate basic vocabulary and grammatical structures, and with intelligible pronunciation.  Exchanges simple information on programming tools and solutions to a client's requirement."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n2le02ob01re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Simple Past Tense in Affirmative Statements with Both Regular and Irregular Verbs"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use <span class=\"strong\">simple past </span>when you want to talk about an action that started and finished in the past. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Verbs in English can be <span class=\"strong\"> regular </span> or <span class=\"strong\"> irregular </span>, you can identify them more easily when they are in <span class=\"strong\"> simple past</span>. With <span class=\"strong\"> regular verbs </span> you just have to add <span class=\"strong\">\u2013ed </span> at the end. On the other hand, <span class=\"strong\"> irregular verbs </span> have a complex change in their structure and must be learned by practice and use."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\"> We <span class=\"strong\"> talked </span> last night about the OOP. <br/> I <span class=\"strong\"> learnt </span>